Z||000000||ponya||12-31-2000||03:16 PM||||As you know, I have been having my share of brake problems latley. I read a current post about brakes locking up. The rotors on my car were recently cut and I was told that they were just within tolerance. Could this be a reason that my front brakes don't lock up? I never did like the fact that they were borderline usable. Any thoughts appreciated.   Thanks, Bob||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||12-31-2000||06:00 PM||||I would have to say that its more of a hydraulic problem.  The piston can travel as far as it needs to, so it is not like that extra .001 of an inch or so that you dont have on your rotor is causing the piston not to be able to get the pad to make it all the way.<BR>  Did you ever open up the brake system while working on brakes, i.e., did you take the brake lines off your calipers?  If you did, you must bleed the air out of the brakes.  If you've already bled the brakes, so the pressure is at its maxium potential (no air in sys.) then I can only speculate that maybe the master cyl. could be causing something queer.<BR>  I can lock up all 4 on my coupe.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||12-31-2000||06:07 PM||||Ponya,<BR>Are you using CarboMet pads?<BR>I put a set of those on my Formula, and I can't lock the wheels, either. Very hard pedal.<BR>Once they get hot, they work a bit better, but I still can't make 'em lock up.||||reg||1
